  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nToday through Friday...\n\nHigh pressure in northern IL will slide southeast today and \ngradually slide east across the southern Great Lakes region  \nthrough today. The core of the surface high will shift to the \nNE US but the high pressure will remain entrenched much further\nwest across WI. In the upper levels we will remain in between \nthe ridge to the west and trough to the east. While there should\nbe some activity aloft with various shortwaves the conditions \nwill be much too dry for anything to come of the upper level \nforcing. Though we will some diurnal cumulus given \n\nThe high pressure will finally start to exit Friday with lower \npressure pushing in from the west as the ridge aloft nudges \ntoward the region. No real weather concerns for this period as \nwe remain dry.\n\nIt is worth mentioning that Beach Hazards Statement is now in\neffect for Friday late afternoon and evening for Door County as\nbreezy onshore winds may yield nearshore wave heights from 3-5 \nfeet. Expansion to the south may be needed.\n\nFriday night through Wednesday...\n\nAs the ridge nudges into the region this weekend expect \ntemperatures to warm back up with a return of humid conditions, \nbut also a return of precip/storm chances. Saturday will carry \nminor chances across far northern WI late Friday night into the\nday Saturday with a weak embedded shortwave sliding down the \nridge. There currently looks to be enough moisture and forcing \nto bring some precip with some instability in the region likely \nallowing for some storms as well. The chance for stronger storms\nis really predicated on whether these storms have enough lift to\nreach the LFC but if any do, 500-1500 J/kg of instability \npaired with 40-50kts of 0-6km shear suggests we could at least\ngets some hailers though given the timing storms would likely be\nelevated.\n\nSunday and Monday will be warmer with highs in the upper 80s to\nlow 90s with multiple chances for storms for northern WI where\nSunday will feature a warm front (strong WAA) as the primary \nforcing should allow for high instability and a decent LLJ will \nbring in enough shear to bring some severe weather concerns. \nInstability will climb to at least 2000-3000 J/kg with strong \ndeep layer shear of 40-55kts with most of the low level helicity\ncoming in the lowest 1 km. There will be additional chances for\nMonday with very similar conditions. However, the uncertainty\nremains fairly high for this period with models kind of all over\nthe place regarding placement of the forcings and the initiation\nof potential convection but generally both days have a chance\nfor strong to severe storms but the details are still fuzzy on\nexactly what could be expected. Otherwise increased uncertainty\ntoward midweek but the upper levels generally show the ridge\nreverting west a tad with weaker high pressure over the region\nand several upper level shortwaves rolling through. This\nsuggests that things could very easily become active for this\nperiod despite limited potential shown in models at this time as\nno feature truly stands out at this time.",
